ison, with each category split into ten levels. A demo version limits players to three levels per category, and the listing says it was tested on Windows 7 and later.\n\nThe content is tightly focused, so it works as a simple practice tool rather than a broad game. It is single-player, and the publisher presents it as suitable for any age group. It fits parents or teachers who want an uncomplicated way for learners to rehearse number skills, and anyone who prefers short, clearly organized exercises to open-ended play."}}